\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A GUI agent outputs a coordinate for a machine to click; we train a small vision-language
model for a different consumer, a person who must be told in one sentence which on-screen
element to touch. We fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B with QLoRA on $64{,}567$ steps labelled with
no human annotation, and score $4{,}463$ held-out touch steps by \emph{executability}.
The sentence goes to an independent grounding model that never sees the gold coordinate,
and the step is correct when the returned point falls in the screen region nearest the
element the user touched. We calibrate that instrument at both ends. Human references reach
$75.7\%$, a contentless sentence scores $12.0\%$, and a fluent, correctly-styled sentence
written for a \emph{different} screen scores $6.1\%$, below the contentless floor on
disjoint intervals, so the grounder reads content and not register. Read on that scale,
automatic supervision works and it replicates. Executability rises from $47.6\%$ to
$59.4\%$ over two seeds, $22$ times the $0.52$ pp seed-to-seed difference, and the sign
holds under a second, independently trained grounder. Whether it also helps to name the
element to be touched in a structured slot before writing is a pre-registered ablation we
could not complete. That branch scores $57.2\%$ at its single seed, $1.93$ points below its
matched control, but its second seed was never run, and the registered thresholds leave the
rest of the outcome space at inconclusive or negative, so no positive result is withheld.
Scored against gold labels, the descriptor prefix adds $5.7$ points where the model identifies the
element correctly and subtracts $25.2$ where it does not, while the human references still
reach $66.3\%$ on those same steps. A third of the difference sits in the $7.3\%$ of steps
emitting no descriptor, where the untuned model names the correct action class on $83.4\%$
against $55.1\%$ and $38.8\%$ for the two trained branches, a failure fine-tuning
introduces before any descriptor exists. A preference stage aimed at identification adds
$+0.63$ pp over a matched control, inconclusive again, with $78\%$ of that movement
belonging to the control. Descriptor-first supervision is not uniformly inert.
What it is worth is conditional on identification accuracy.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
GUI instruction generation, vision-language models, referring expression generation,
reference-free evaluation, pre-registration
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Given a mobile screenshot and a user goal, a GUI agent predicts an action such as
\texttt{click(872,141)}. A different consumer of the same input is a person who needs to
be told what to do, for example \emph{tap the pencil icon at the top right of the note}. Systems
serving that consumer are appearing in accessibility and user-support
settings~\cite{guideme}, but the generation problem has had little quantitative treatment.
A recorded touch coordinate can be checked against a predicted one; a sentence cannot be
checked against a single reference, since many wordings identify the same button and no
dataset enumerates them.

This paper is a model study and needs an instrument before it can be one. We use
\emph{executability}. The generated sentence goes to a separate grounding
model~\cite{uground} that never sees the gold coordinate, and the step counts as correct
when that model lands in the \emph{cell} of the element the user touched, the screen region
nearest it, and the action class agrees. Section~\ref{sec:metric} gives the calibration that licenses it --- adequacy gate,
measured ceiling, measured floor, sensitivity over five hit rules --- in compressed form.

We then ask two model questions, in the order they should be asked.

\emph{The first question is whether automatically constructed supervision works at all.}
We build training
targets from AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} with no human annotation and no
labels generated by another language model, and fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B~\cite{qwen25vl}
with QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora}. It does. Executability rises from $47.6$ to $59.4$ points at
two seeds, and the gap survives six alternative explanations, one of which discards every
sentence that reproduces its reference verbatim.

\emph{The second question is whether it helps to name the element before speaking.}
The referring-expression
tradition says a description is good when a listener recovers the referent from
it~\cite{mao2016,luo2017,yu2017}, and the residual errors of the fine-tuned model are of
that kind. Of its $1{,}825$ failures, $86\%$ carry the correct action together with a
wording the grounder cannot resolve. So we registered a branch whose target is a structured descriptor
--- role, name, coordinate, distinguishing cue --- followed by the same scored sentence,
byte for byte, as the control. The registered estimand averages two seeds per branch; one
of the four runs was not executed and will not be, so it is permanently incomplete. At the
single seed available the treatment sits $1.93$ points below its control, inside the band
declared inconclusive. The mechanism is nonetheless legible. The component works where the model
identifies the element correctly and costs heavily where it does not, and the largest locus
of that cost is a failure fine-tuning introduces before any descriptor exists. A second
registered stage, a minimal-pair preference objective aimed at identification itself, was
then run against a matched supervised control. It adds $+0.63$ pp, inside the inconclusive
band again, with $78\%$ of the stage's gain belonging to the control.

Our contributions are: (i) an instrument for this task calibrated at both ends, with a
measured ceiling of $75.7\%$ and, less usually, a \emph{measured floor}, a contentless
sentence scoring $12.0\%$ and a wrong-screen one $6.1\%$ on disjoint intervals; (ii) a
fully automatic supervision pipeline for GUI instruction generation, its gain measured at
two seeds and replicated under a second, independently trained grounder; (iii) a
pre-registered ablation of descriptor-first supervision, reported against its own
registered reading rules including the case where those rules refuse a conclusion, together
with the outcome space that remained; and (iv) a diagnosis, exploratory and at one seed, of
the condition that governs what the prefix is worth, of the failure fine-tuning itself
introduces before any descriptor exists, and of what the follow-up objective returned.

\section{Related Work}

\emph{GUI grounding and agents.} AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} provides
$15{,}283$ human-recorded episodes with per-step instructions and gold touch
coordinates; AndroidInTheWild~\cite{aitw} is the source of the $14\%$ tolerance
convention. Grounding models such as SeeClick~\cite{seeclick}, OS-Atlas~\cite{osatlas},
UGround~\cite{uground} and UI-Venus~\cite{uivenus} map a text expression to a screen
point; we use two as instruments, not as baselines. In Aguvis~\cite{aguvis} a model emits a low-level
instruction, but only as an intermediate representation en route to an action, whose
quality is never scored. In AndroidControl the human-written per-step instruction
is an \emph{input} conditioning action prediction; here it is the generation target and
the only scored output. Supplying a structured element list is likewise established ---
the AndroidControl authors fine-tune with an accessibility-derived list as input,
Mind2Web~\cite{mind2web} trains over noisy candidates, Widget
Captioning~\cite{widgetcap} puts the view hierarchy at the input --- so we claim no
novelty for that layer.

\emph{Discriminative description.} Producing a description that identifies one object
among distractors is referring-expression generation~\cite{mao2016}, and later work
places a comprehension model inside the training loop to enforce
discriminativeness~\cite{luo2017,yu2017}. The closest supervised work in the GUI domain is
Widget Captioning~\cite{widgetcap}, which identifies the problem of visually identical
widgets but trains with plain cross-entropy. Our claim is narrow. We place
discriminativeness in the \emph{training target} for GUI instructions and measure it. Outside the GUI domain, making a model emit grounding coordinates before it answers helps
when \emph{trained} rather than prompted~\cite{gcot}, the closest prior to our branch and
the expectation our result should be read against. Inside the GUI domain,
removing the inner monologue from the Aguvis training targets costs $11.4$ points on
AndroidControl-Low, our subtraction from their ablation table~\cite{aguvis} --- an intermediate representation consumed by
an action head, not a sentence handed to a person, so it sets an expectation without
settling it.

\emph{Reference-free evaluation.} Verifying generated text against an external
structured source rather than the generator's own beliefs is the principle behind
ALOHa~\cite{aloha}; perturbation checklists with pre-declared thresholds are an
established way of validating a metric~\cite{sai2021}. Two cautions apply. Automatic
proxies for human judgement are easily over-trusted~\cite{clark2021}, and grounder
accuracy measured on one ``best'' instruction per element overstates what these models can
do~\cite{groundersunderstand}. The term \emph{executability} is used differently in open
grounded planning~\cite{ogp}, as the share of plans whose every action appears in a given
action library --- a symbolic lookup over text. Ours is behavioural and visual.

\section{Task, Supervision, and Branches}
\label{sec:data}

Given one screenshot $I$ and a user goal $q$, the task is to produce a single instruction
$s$ in natural language that identifies the element to touch and the action to perform, and
that is addressed to a human reader.

\emph{Supervision.} No public redistribution of AndroidControl carries the human-written
instruction, the screenshot and the accessibility tree together, so we join two mirrors on
\texttt{(episode\_id, step\_id)} and take trees for $99{,}131$ screens from the same
release. An off-by-one join would corrupt every downstream label while leaving the pipeline
functional, so we test the alignment. OCR text at the gold
touch point appears in the reference on $48\%$ of $n=400$ sampled steps, against $20\%$
under a deliberately shifted control. The corpus holds $64{,}567$ training and $6{,}958$ test steps over $12{,}895$ and $1{,}432$
tasks, about $64\%$ of each being touch steps and forming the scoring population; the split
is held out by task with zero overlap, verified at full scale. On a random sample of $120$ of those screens only
$12.6\%$ of elements carry any name, a shortfall reported elsewhere too~\cite{chen2020}, so
every screenshot is passed through OCR, used as an input feature and as a name source. The descriptor label at each touch
step is built by rule from the smallest accessibility box containing the gold point:
role from the tree, name from the tree when it passes a validity gate and from OCR
inside the box otherwise, the gold point on a $[0,1000]$ grid, and a distinguishing cue.
At full scale $73.6\%$ of labels carry a clear name, $22.0\%$ none, and $7.6\%$ of
target elements share their name with another element on the same screen. The
construction, its two filtering gates and its label-quality audit are the subject of a
companion paper~\cite{companionvcl}; we use its output here.

\emph{Branches.} Four datasets are built from identical inputs and differ only in the
generation target: \textbf{S1}, the sentence alone; \textbf{S2}, descriptor then
sentence; \textbf{S2r}, a false descriptor taken from a different screen with
randomised coordinates, token-length matched per example; and \textbf{S2-nopoint}, S2
without the coordinate slot. An S2 target reads
\texttt{<desc>tappable text | Search here | <point>440,91</point> | just above the text
"Coffee"</desc>} followed by the same scored sentence as S1. Nine structural invariants are checked
after every rebuild and all nine hold at full scale. The one that matters most here is that
the scored sentence is byte-identical across branches on all $64{,}567$ examples, so a
difference between branches is a difference in what precedes the sentence and nothing
else. S2r and S2-nopoint were registered as
attribution controls and, for the same reason as the second treatment seed
(Section~\ref{sec:design}), were not
run; we mark them as registered-and-unrun rather than dropping them from the record.

\section{Descriptor-First Supervision}
\label{sec:method}

The intervention is a change of target, not of architecture, input or inference cost.
Both branches read the same prompt --- goal, the reference instructions of the previous
three steps, and the OCR strings --- and both are scored on the sentence alone, with any
descriptor stripped before scoring. S2 differs only in that the target begins with four
slots: \emph{role} (button, text field, list item), \emph{name} (the displayed string),
\emph{coordinate} (the gold touch point rescaled to $[0,1000]$), and a
\emph{distinguishing cue} (the nearest OCR string outside the box with its relative
direction).

Two design choices are not free. The coordinate slot is included because it is a clean
label at zero construction cost, and the evidence at design time indicated that
intermediate steps carrying an explicit point help where free-form verbal reasoning hurts.
The descriptor is also emitted rather than consumed, so the model must produce its own
identification before writing, which makes the branch an ablation of \emph{discriminative
commitment} rather than of extra input. That commitment cuts both ways, as Section~\ref{sec:diag}
tests. A model that has just written the right name and point is well placed to write a
sentence about that element, while one that has written the wrong ones has bound itself to
them.

\section{The Instrument}
\label{sec:metric}

\subsection{Definition}
Let $s$ be the generated sentence with any descriptor removed, $I$ the screenshot, $g$
the gold touch coordinate, and $E$ the set of accessibility elements on the screen after
merging elements whose centres lie within $24$\,dp ($63$ px at the modal density) of one
another or of $g$. Let $G$ be a grounding model,
independent of the model under test and without access to $g$, returning $p = G(I,s)$.
The step is correct when all three hold: \emph{(i) action}, the canonicalised action
types of $s$ and of the reference agree, twelve surface verbs mapping to a single touch
class, with touch, type, scroll, long-press and back kept distinct (with one leak,
quantified in Section~\ref{sec:alt}); \emph{(ii) polarity},
$s$ and the reference do not name opposite states of the same control, checked against
nine toggle pairs; \emph{(iii) localisation}, $p$ lies within the conventional
tolerance $|p_x - g_x| \le 0.14W$, $|p_y - g_y| \le 0.14H$~\cite{aitw} \emph{and} no
competing element centre is closer to $p$ than $g$ itself. The tolerance region is a
per-axis rectangle, not a disc ($\pm151$ px horizontally against $\pm336$ px vertically
on a $1080\times2400$ screen), and the point that defines the cell is the recorded touch
coordinate, not the centre of the box containing it. Condition (iii) refines the tolerance
test and never accepts a step that test rejects, so the two are not alternatives.
Executability is the proportion of touch steps satisfying all three.

The rubric is a recorded human touch coordinate, not a model's opinion, so this is not
LLM-as-judge. $G$ contributes a localisation and geometry against $g$ decides, and the same
$G$ scores every branch, which removes any bias acting equally on all of them.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\columnwidth]{fig_voronoi.png}
\caption{Why the tolerance test alone cannot carry the judgement. The user touched the
\emph{Birthday} row; its cell is shaded and the fine-tuned model's sentence sends the
grounder to the cross inside it. The untuned model wrote ``Click on the `Gender'
section\ldots'' and the grounder went where it was told, $128$ px away on the row above but
inside the $\pm336$ px vertical tolerance, which the conventional rule scores correct and
the cell rule does not. Circles are competing element centres.}
\label{fig:cell}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:cell} shows a step on which the two rules disagree. The choice is not
cosmetic. With the grounder's answer displaced to the centre of the nearest competing
element, the tolerance test still awards credit in $84.3\%$ of cases (injection study,
$250$ steps), a dynamic range of about $16$ points, where the cell rule awards $2.8\%$
while holding a ceiling of $99.7\%$ under a $3\%$ injected offset. We report the tolerance
score alongside the headline throughout, never as the headline.

\subsection{Calibration}
Four measurements license the readings in this paper.

\emph{Adequacy.} A pass condition of median localisation error at most $3\%$ of screen
width was fixed before any compute was spent, derived from screen geometry. The tolerance
margin, half the distance from the element to be touched to the nearest other element after
merging, has median $6.2\%$ of width and 10th percentile $3.6\%$. UGround-V1-2B
attains $0.7\%$ on $300$ test steps. The gate passes, but the error distribution is
bimodal (the grounder either lands near the centre or elsewhere entirely), so a percentile
condition would have been the better choice, and one declared check for a broken
installation fired. On $30.7\%$ of steps the horizontal answer is exactly screen centre,
part of that in the data and the rest the grounder abandoning the axis when it cannot
identify an element. Both depress scores rather than inflate them.

\emph{Ceiling.} Passing every human-written reference through the same pipeline scores
$\mathbf{75.7\%}$ $[74.1, 77.3]$ over all $4{,}463$ touch steps. Every score below is read
against $75.7$, not against $100$. An earlier $300$-step estimate of $70.0\%$ was five
points low and is withdrawn as a ceiling.

\emph{Floor.} Replacing every sentence with the contentless \emph{tap the button} scores
$12.0\%$ $[9.7, 14.4]$ on a random $800$-step slice whose ceiling is $74.9\%$, so the
usable range is $62.9$ points and the metric is not carried by what the grounder can guess
from the screenshot alone. A
genuine reference sentence written for a \emph{different} step --- correct register,
matched length, wrong content --- scores lower still, $6.1\%$ $[4.5, 7.9]$, on disjoint
intervals. A wrong sentence actively misleads the instrument, where an empty one leaves it
to a weak prior. This also disposes of the reading that the metric rewards annotator style, which is exactly
what that control preserves.

\emph{What the metric weighs.} On the same slice, keeping the locative clause and
replacing the element name with the generic \emph{the item} costs $28.5$ points on the
$193$ steps it touches ($b{=}3$ steps the shortened sentence resolves and the reference
does not, $c{=}58$ the reverse, $p<0.001$), against $3.5$ points for deleting
the locative clause and keeping the name on a near-identical population of $198$
($b{=}1$, $c{=}8$, $p{=}0.046$). Naming the element is worth roughly eight times saying
where it is. Meaning-preserving rewrites of the references --- verb substitution,
fronting the locative clause, both at once --- move executability by $+0.35$ pp
($[-0.59, +1.29]$) over the $1{,}139$ steps they alter, with $2.6\%$ of steps changing
verdict, so the instrument is insensitive to these rewrites while being sensitive to content.

\emph{Why not a reference-based score.} Scored with a content-word F1 against the single
reference, $52.8\%$ of the sentences the metric accepts from the untuned model fall below
$0.5$, against $13.9\%$ for the fine-tuned one. A reference-based score therefore charges
the two branches unequally for register, a property unrelated to identification. BLEU-4 and
ROUGE-L rank the branches in the same order, but we draw nothing from that and neither can
locate the remaining headroom, scoring the references $100$ by construction where
executability puts them at $75.7$.

\subsection{Robustness of the instrument}
Choosing a hit rule is a researcher degree of freedom, so we measured what it buys.
Rescoring three branches on $698$ steps under five rules (Euclidean disc, per-axis
rectangle, the cell rule as implemented, a cell rule seeded at the box centre, and a
nearest-box rule) gives ceilings of $80.2$, $82.2$, $74.2$, $56.6$ and $82.2$, and a
fine-tuning gain of $+12.6$, $+13.0$, $+11.7$, $+9.5$ and $+13.0$ points. The absolute level
moves by up to $26$ points, the ordering never does, and the gain stays between $9.5$ and
$13.0$. The instrument contributes no variance of its own. On the $2{,}810$ steps
where two training runs emitted byte-identical sentences, the grounder returned identical
coordinates and identical verdicts on all $2{,}810$.

Intervals are nonparametric cluster bootstraps with $10{,}000$ resamples, clustered by
application where one can be attributed and by task otherwise ($G = 1{,}091$, of which
$259$ are application clusters and $832$ single-task clusters covering $59\%$ of steps;
$G_{\text{eff}} = 454.3$ under Kish's formula~\cite{kish1965}). The rule was fixed in
advance because it determines the available power~\cite{cameron2008}. The measured design
effect is $1.10$; standard errors are contrast-specific, $0.785$ pp for
$\mathrm{S1} - \mathrm{Base}$, giving a minimum detectable paired effect of $2.2$ points
at $80\%$ power. That prices instrument noise alone; a contrast read across two
\emph{training runs} also carries the seed term of Section~\ref{sec:design}.

\section{Experimental Design and Pre-Registration}
\label{sec:design}

All branches fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct~\cite{qwen25vl} with
QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora} under LLaMA-Factory~\cite{llamafactory}: 4-bit NF4, LoRA rank $8$,
$\alpha = 16$, dropout $0.05$, adapters on \texttt{q,k,v,o,gate,up,down}, vision tower and
projector frozen, context $2560$, effective batch $16$, learning rate $1\times10^{-4}$,
cosine schedule, two epochs, on a single A100 ($8{,}072$ steps, $\approx 23$ h). One
configuration file serves every branch, four keys changing in it (dataset, seed, output
directory, dataloader workers), and each run archives the file it used. The trainable
parameter count is $14{,}966{,}784$, confirming the vision tower stayed frozen.
Losses on an L4 and an A100 agree to three digits with identical \texttt{total\_flos}, and
generation is byte-identical at batch size $1$ and $8$.

Inference is greedy in every branch, one sentence per step, with the descriptor stripped by
a fixed regular expression before scoring so that no branch is scored on text the others
cannot produce. The untuned model reads the same prompt template as the rest, made of the
goal, the human-written instructions of the previous three steps, and the OCR strings, and
that template is byte-identical across branches and between training and inference. Because the history carries human-written sentences rather than an action log, scoring is
teacher-forced on context. Absolute scores are conditioned on that and branch differences
are not.

The design was committed to version control before any training run and fixes the reading
of all four outcomes in advance: a $\Delta$ whose interval excludes zero and exceeds the
seed noise floor is positive; one whose interval touches zero is a trend; an interval
covering zero with $|\Delta|$ below the detectable effect is inconclusive; a negative upper
bound is harm. The registered quantity is
$\Delta = \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S2}) - \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S1})$ over \emph{two seeds
per branch}, the operative threshold set at $2.8$ pp --- the bootstrap standard error of
the seed pair, $0.38$ pp, with a seed term of $0.46$ pp doubled because it rests on
one degree of freedom --- and the inconclusive band at $-2.8 \ldots +1.7$ pp. That $2.8$ pp
is the band rule for $\mathrm{S2} - \mathrm{S1}$ and is deliberately conservative. To read
the stage-2 pair $\mathrm{MIN} - \mathrm{CE2}$, Section~\ref{sec:mindesc} uses the
undoubled counterpart of $2.11$ pp, which fits a design carrying \emph{one} seed per branch
where the seed term enters as $\sqrt{2}\,\sigma$; the registered two-seed figure is $1.67$
pp, and the verdict is the same under all three. Amendments are
appended with dates, never edited in place; twenty-seven are recorded, twenty-one before
any executability score existed. We flag rather than hide the pattern in the six that came
later. Each of them moved the headroom upward and the threshold downward, and the first
also moved against us, cutting the control branch's share of the ceiling from $84.4\%$ to
$78.0\%$.

\textbf{The second seed of the treatment branch was never run}, a budget decision taken on
23 August 2026, and it will not be. The registered estimand is permanently incomplete, and no later analysis upgrades the
single-seed comparison into the conclusion the two-seed rule would have licensed.

