\subsection{Automatic supervision works, and the effect is replicated}
Fine-tuning on supervision that cost no human annotation moves the model from $47.6$ to $59.4$ points, closing $42\%$ of the distance
to the human-reference level. Paired over the same steps (McNemar), S1 over Base is
$+11.5$ points at seed 101 ($b = 284$, $c = 798$, $\chi^2 = 243.2$) and $+12.0$ at seed
202, both $p<0.001$, where $b$ counts steps the lower-scoring branch resolves and the
higher-scoring one does not.

All rates use the same denominator of $4{,}463$ touch steps, and the three empty sentences,
one per S1 seed and one from S2, are scored zero rather than dropped, since a refusal to
answer is what the metric exists to catch.

The $59.4$ is flattered by the quarter of steps on which S1 reproduces the reference's
content words. Restricted to the $3{,}367$ steps where the model writes its own sentence,
the branches read $44.7$, $53.1$ and $75.0$, the fraction closed is $28\%$, and the gap is
still $+8.4$ points ($b=264$, $c=547$, $p<0.001$). Five further readings of the per-step record leave the ordering intact. Localisation alone
leaves $+11.3$ points, and the untuned model names the correct action class \emph{more}
often ($96.5\%$ against $94.4\%$). The gain is $+15.0$ points on a task's
opening step against $+11.0$ afterwards, on overlapping intervals, and by screen difficulty
in thirds of element count it is $+12.6$, $+9.5$ and $+12.4$. Per-cluster, S1 wins $395$, ties $565$
and loses $131$, reproduced at the second seed. Application overlap between training and test brings no advantage either. S1 scores $59.1\%$ on applications seen in
training, $59.0\%$ on unseen ones and $59.2\%$ where none can be attributed.

\emph{Retraining noise.} A gap between systems must be read against the movement caused by
rebuilding one. Retraining S1 under a second seed shifts the score by $+0.52$
points, a paired interval of $[-0.2, +1.3]$ containing zero, McNemar $p = 0.19$. The
sharper contrast is in how many individual verdicts change. The seeds disagree on $6.4\%$
of steps, where the intervention moves $24.2\%$. Step-level agreement across seeds is $93.6\%$ ($\kappa = 0.867$), but
$63\%$ of those pairs are byte-identical sentences a deterministic instrument must agree
on; on the $1{,}652$ steps where the runs wrote something different it is $82.6\%$
($\kappa = 0.650$), the figure we quote.

\subsection{A second grounder}
\label{sec:venus}
Every number above is conditioned on one grounder, trained on $47$K elements from the
AndroidControl training split and so familiar with the register S1 is trained to imitate.
We re-scored three branches on a $2{,}532$-step slice with
UI-Venus-Ground-7B~\cite{uivenus}, not trained on AndroidControl (Table~\ref{tab:venus}).

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Two grounders; contrasts on the same $2{,}532$-step slice. The positive control
$\mathrm{S1}-\mathrm{Base}$, an effect already established under the first grounder,
retains $94\%$ of its size, so the scale is not compressed. That row is raw on the slice;
the $\mathrm{S2}-\mathrm{S1}$ row is rescaled to the $4{,}463$-step denominator used
elsewhere. The last row is the $300$-step adequacy sample scored by each grounder, for
comparing the instruments only; the full-scale reference ceiling is $75.7$.}
\label{tab:venus}
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Contrast & UGround & UI-Venus \\
\midrule
S1 $-$ Base (pos.\ control) & $+10.35\,[+8.39, +12.40]$ & $+9.68\,[+7.60, +11.78]$ \\
S2 $-$ S1 (single seed) & $-1.93\,[-3.08, -0.78]$   & $-1.21\,[-2.30, -0.12]$ \\
\midrule
Adequacy ceiling ($n{=}300$) & $70.0\,[64.5, 75.3]$      & $69.3\,[63.8, 74.8]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The positive control keeps $94\%$ of its magnitude and the two adequacy ceilings are within
$0.7$ points, so the explanation that fine-tuning merely teaches the annotator's register,
a register the first grounder was trained on, does not survive. The sign of the S2 contrast
holds too, though the two scorings share checkpoints and so do not substitute for the
missing seed. A paired difference-in-differences puts that contrast $+1.26$ pp less
negative under UI-Venus, $[+0.04, +2.51]$. That evidence is weak and exploratory, and it
cannot carry the reading that the first grounder is what makes S2 lose, which would require
the contrast to reach zero. Part of the shrinkage is mechanical, since UI-Venus scores
$2.8$--$4.1$ points lower on every branch.

\subsection{The descriptor branch}
The registered comparison is $\mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S2}) - \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S1})$
over two seeds each. With the treatment at one seed, only a narrower statement is available.
\emph{With Qwen2.5-VL-3B under QLoRA and the present automatic labelling pipeline, the
S2/101 checkpoint scores $1.93$ points below S1/101 ($b = 254$, $c = 340$, interval
$[-3.06, -0.75]$) and $2.44$ points below S1/202 over the full $4{,}463$ steps, the sign
holds under UI-Venus, and the registered estimand remains incomplete.} Against the declared
bands, $-2.19$ pp relative to the two-seed control mean falls inside $-2.8 \ldots +1.7$:
\emph{inconclusive}, not a negative result, and the missing run means it stays there. The
seed noise on the control pair, $\pm1.3$ pp at the interval edge, is most of the reason.

What the missing run could have changed is fixed by the registered thresholds. To bring the
two-seed mean to the weakly-positive edge of $\Delta = +1.7$ pp it would have had to score
$65.0\%$, which is $4.9$ points above the best checkpoint we have measured, MIN at $60.1$,
and $16.9$ seed-to-seed standard deviations away; for the negative band it would have had to
score at or below $56.0\%$, $2.7$ such deviations away. The remaining outcome space was
\emph{inconclusive or negative}, and the decision closed it at inconclusive; we report this
rather than let a reader infer a suppressed positive.

Two things do not depend on the missing seed. The branch is far above the untuned model,
$+9.6$ points $[+8.2, +11.1]$, so the descriptor target does not break fine-tuning, and it
emits its coordinate slot well, $71.0\%$ of the points it generates on steps with a
parsable descriptor landing inside the $14\%$ window with a median horizontal error of
$0.81\%$ of screen width. Whatever it costs, it does not cost the ability to point.

\subsection{A preference objective at the descriptor layer}
\label{sec:mindesc}
Section~\ref{sec:diag} points at identification accuracy rather than at the prefix, so we
ran the objective registered for it, MIN. From the S2 checkpoint it applies $800$ further updates on minimal pairs whose accepted side is the gold descriptor followed by the
sentence and whose rejected side is a wrong descriptor followed by the \emph{same sentence,
verbatim}, so the preference term can be reduced only by choosing the right element. Its
attribution control, CE2, is plain supervised training on the accepted side alone, with the
same checkpoint, the same $800$ updates, the same schedule and no preference term.

Both branches are in Table~\ref{tab:main}. The primary registered quantity is the paired
difference between them, which is $+0.63$ pp, $[+0.16, +1.10]$, with $b = 42$, $c = 70$,
$\chi^2 = 6.5$ and $p = 0.011$. That interval carries instrument noise only, its own cluster-bootstrap
standard error being $0.24$ pp; the minimum detectable effect for a design carrying one
seed per branch, computed in advance from the $0.38$ pp measured on the seed pair together
with the $0.46$ pp seed term of Section~\ref{sec:design}, is $2.11$ pp, and $+0.63$ is
$1.4\times$ that seed term. By the reading rule fixed
before any score existed, this is the \emph{inconclusive} band. Measurement noise is
separable here and training noise is not.

The preference branch is $+2.87$ pp above
S2 $[+2.03, +3.76]$, but $+2.24$ $[+1.41, +3.11]$ of that is bought by the control --- $800$
further supervised updates, no preference term anywhere --- and only $+0.63$ sits on top.
In this pair $78\%$ of the movement belongs to the control branch, and reporting $+2.87$ as
the effect of the objective would overstate it more than fourfold. A steeper
split appears
one layer down, at the descriptor, where the control lifts joint name-and-point accuracy by
$+5.9$ pp against $+0.8$ pp for the preference term, a split of $88/12$. Nor does the stage overtake plain sentence
supervision. MIN $-$ S1 is $+0.94$ $[-0.09, +2.05]$, $p = 0.11$, and CE2 $-$ S1 is $+0.31$,
$p = 0.61$. The stage repairs the damage the descriptor target did to itself, then rises a
little above it.

A descriptor-to-executability conversion factor of $0.43$, estimated post hoc on these same
checkpoints, predicts $+0.35$ pp here against the $+0.63$ measured, a consistency check
rather than a registered prediction.

\emph{A registered variant the data refused.} The rejected side needs a source, and two
were registered: the heuristic one used here, the nearest same-role element $80$--$350$ px
away, and an on-policy one taking the element the checkpoint itself names when it names the
wrong one. The second was stopped by its feasibility gate before any training, $14{,}000$
training screens giving $459$ usable pairs, $3.3\%$ against a threshold of $25\%$, and the
reason is measurable. Over the $3{,}246$ steps carrying a wrong name, the distance from the
element named to the element to be touched has quartiles of $70$, $351$ and $748$ px, so
the window rejects $76.5\%$ of them. These errors fall into two kinds, the right element
under another name or a different region of the screen, and neither is the local confusion
an on-policy negative presumes. The heuristic source therefore does not sample these errors
so much as construct a neighbour-confusion setting the data barely contains. We report both
sources because the measurement, not a preference, left the heuristic one standing.

\section{Where the Descriptor Helps and Where It Breaks}
\label{sec:diag}

This section is exploratory and rests on one treatment seed. We report it because the
mechanism it exposes is what a follow-up objective must act on, and because two of its
components were registered before any treatment score existed.

\subsection{The component works when identification succeeds}
The branch emits its own descriptor, so its identification can be scored against the gold
labels independently of the sentence. Table~\ref{tab:cross} crosses name against coordinate
correctness on the $3{,}245$ steps that carry a gold name and receive a parsable descriptor;
the other $228$ emit none (Section~\ref{sec:noslice}).

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Executability by whether the branch's own descriptor got the name and the
coordinate right. Base and the human references are scored on the same steps without being
conditioned on the treatment, so they indicate how hard each row is. The stratification is
post-hoc, bounded by the Ceil. column rather than a causal claim. Cluster-bootstrap
intervals on $\Delta$ clear zero in the first and last rows only, $[+4.5, +7.0]$ and
$[-28.5, -21.9]$.}
\label{tab:cross}
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr}
\toprule
Name & Point & $n$ & Base & S1 & S2 & $\Delta$ & Ceil. \\
\midrule
correct & inside  & $1{,}871$ & 69.0 & 81.4 & \textbf{87.1} & $\mathbf{+5.7}$ & 86.6 \\
correct & outside & $184$     & 23.4 & 25.5 & 26.1 & $+0.5$ & 31.0 \\
wrong   & inside  & $452$     & 41.4 & 56.4 & 57.5 & $+1.1$ & 72.8 \\
wrong   & outside & $738$     & 24.4 & 30.8 & \textbf{5.6} & $\mathbf{-25.2}$ & 66.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The branch wins or ties in three rows of four. All the damage sits in the fourth. With both the
name wrong and the coordinate outside tolerance, on $738$ steps or $16.5\%$ of the
$4{,}463$, executability falls to $5.6\%$. The cost of the design is commitment. Having named the wrong element, the branch writes a sentence faithful
to it, while the control branch, committed to nothing, still retrieves the right one three
times in ten.

The objection is that this stratification conditions on a variable the treatment itself
produces, so the failing row may simply be the hard steps. The two rightmost columns test
that and are not conditioned on the treatment. If the row were merely hard, the references
would fall with it, yet they reach $66.3\%$ there, $9$ points below their $75.7$, where
the treatment reaches $5.6$ and the untuned model $24.4$. The row is harder than average
and that does not account for the collapse; the comparison is not clean, since a correct
test needs a difficulty measure fixed before the treatment ran, but the confound is bounded
rather than merely acknowledged. One row does behave as the objection predicts.
The row \emph{name correct, point outside}, with a ceiling of $31.0\%$, is a region the
instrument largely cannot see, and the near-zero $\Delta$ there is an absence of signal
rather than of effect.

The decomposition of the total is arithmetic and carries no such caveat. The $92.7\%$ of
steps carrying a descriptor contribute $-1.28$ pp and the $7.3\%$ that do not contribute
$-0.65$ pp.

\subsection{A third of the difference sits in $7.3\%$ of steps}
\label{sec:noslice}
The slice separating steps where the branch emits a descriptor from steps where it does
not was registered on 19 August, before any S2 score existed.

\begin{table}[!t]
\caption{Pre-registered activation slice (19 August, before any treatment score).}
\label{tab:activation}
\centering
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Group & $n$ & S1 & S2 & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
Descriptor emitted    & $4{,}138$ & 62.2 & 60.8 & $-1.38\,[-2.54, -0.19]$ \\
No descriptor emitted & $325$     & 19.7 & 10.8 & $-8.92\,[-12.57, -5.25]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The $7.3\%$ of steps where the branch emits nothing carry $34\%$ of the total difference.
There the model misjudges the \emph{action class}. In a typical case the reference says
\emph{Click on the close icon} and the branch writes \emph{Swipe up}. The references score $68.3\%$ there, so
the steps are solvable and the instrument is not blind. Nor is the failure created by the
descriptor. The untuned model names the correct action class on $83.4\%$ of them, against
$55.1\%$ for the control and $38.8\%$ for the treatment, so fine-tuning damages the action
prior and the descriptor target amplifies it. The mechanism we cannot yet test is that descriptors appear
only on touch steps in training, so the model may have learnt ``descriptor
$\Leftrightarrow$ touch'' and, when unsure, dropped it for a non-touch sentence;
S2-nopoint, the branch registered to test that, is among the cancelled runs.
One limitation travels with the slice. The $325$ steps are defined by the treatment's own
behaviour, so ``it does badly there'' is partly definitional. What is not definitional is that the control also scores only $19.7\%$.

\subsection{Four alternative explanations tested and set aside}
\label{sec:alt}
\emph{Length.} The difference is negative on long sentences ($-1.85$) and on short ones
($-2.00$), and the two branches' length distributions all but coincide (median $33$ and
$34$ characters). \emph{Application slice.} It is negative on seen ($-2.25$), unseen
($-6.41$) and unattributable ($-1.59$) steps alike. \emph{Canonicalisation.} The \emph{go back} $\to$ touch collapse in
condition (i) hides exactly this failure mode, $65$ treatment sentences containing
\emph{go back} passing the action check where only $21$ are executable, so we re-ran with
the strict variant registered on 16 August before any treatment score. The difference moves
from $-1.93$ to $-1.86$, all four branches dropping $0.20$--$0.31$ points with the ordering
unchanged. \emph{Label noise.} On the $26$ steps whose descriptor label is OCR
garbage the difference is $+7.69$ ($p = 0.63$), the opposite direction.

\subsection{What the three regimes look like}

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{One held-out step from each regime, verbatim. The descriptor is stripped before
scoring, so only the final sentence is measured.}
\label{tab:qual}
\centering
\scriptsize
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.0}
\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.955\columnwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\emph{(a) Identification succeeds; the prefix pays.} \\
Reference: ``Click on the search icon.'' \\
S1: ``Click on the text 3.'' \hfill \emph{fails} \\
S2: \texttt{<desc>icon button | SEARCH | <point>893,396</point> | the only icon button on
screen</desc>} ``Click on the search icon.'' \hfill \emph{succeeds} \\
\midrule
\emph{(b) Identification fails; the prefix binds the sentence to the error.} \\
Reference: ``Click on BMW option on the screen.'' \\
S1: ``Click on BMW option on the screen.'' \hfill \emph{succeeds} \\
S2: \texttt{<desc>item | (no name) | <point>100,294</point> | just below the text
``Acura''</desc>} ``Click on the first car brand on the screen.'' \hfill \emph{fails} \\
\midrule
\emph{(c) No descriptor emitted; the action class is wrong.} \\
Reference: ``click on the Three lines at the top left corner.'' \\
S1: ``Click on the three lines at the top left corner of the screen.'' \hfill
\emph{succeeds} \\
S2: ``Go back to the previous page.'' \hfill \emph{fails} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

In (b) the descriptor carries no name, the element having none in the accessibility tree
and none inside its box under OCR, and the branch describes a position in a list instead.
That sentence is fluent, faithful to its own descriptor, and unusable. In (c) nothing is
emitted and the sentence is not about a touch. Neither failure is one of writing English.

\subsection{Why this does not simply contradict the grounding-first prior}
The reported gains for targets that ground before answering were not measured on this
task, and three differences matter. Their supervision is a question--answer pair whose
grounded object is what the question is \emph{about}, so a grounding error shows in the
answer, where ours admits a fluent, wrong sentence after a wrong descriptor. Their
grounding target is checked against annotation at training time, ours at inference against
the model's own identification, the asymmetry Table~\ref{tab:cross} finds. And their gains
are read on answer accuracy, whose floor is near chance, while executability runs from a
measured $12.0$ to $75.7$, leaving $62.9$ points in which $2$ points is small. Ours is a boundary condition on that prior rather than a contradiction. The prefix
pays where the model can identify the referent, and identification is harder here, with
$22.0\%$ of the elements to be touched carrying no name in tree or OCR.

\subsection{What this says about the intervention}
The three tables say more than that the ablation did not pay. The supervision is not
uniformly inert. It adds $5.7$ points where the model identifies the element correctly and
subtracts $25$ where identification has failed, so what it is worth is conditional on
identification accuracy rather than on the prefix. Nor is the lever
``remove a slot''. The two middle rows, $+1.1$ $[-2.5, +4.7]$ where only the point is right
and $+0.5$ $[-2.8, +4.3]$ where only the name is, separate neither from zero nor from each
other, so they license no verdict on either slot. The lever is ``make identification reliable''. Lifting the failing row to the control's own
level on those $738$ steps would be worth $4.2$ points,
the size of effect the study seeks. Section~\ref{sec:mindesc} reports the objective
registered for exactly that, and what it returned. Identification accuracy is both the lever
and, so far, what has not moved far enough; choosing this objective \emph{after} seeing the
S2 result is post-hoc and recorded as such.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limits}

\emph{The registered estimand is incomplete, permanently.} The treatment branch has one
seed, and so do both stage-2 branches, so everything said about them is exploratory. We do
not say what the pre-registration forbids: that the hypothesis was refuted, that the
treatment is harmful by the registered quantity, or that a McNemar $p$ settles a question in
which seed variance is a component.

\emph{Ceiling and blind region.} The human-reference level is $75.7\%$, so the headroom for
any intervention is narrower than a score read against $100$ suggests. The references fail
on $1{,}083$ steps, $72\%$ of those because the grounder lands more than $14\%$ of screen
width away on a human sentence, and $935$ steps defeat all three branches. Below the merge radius the
metric is blind, detecting a wrong element named but not a point slightly off inside the
right one.

\emph{Grounder dependence.} Two grounders agree on the ordering, but both are built on
Qwen-family backbones, so a same-family effect is not excluded and no human evaluation
anchors either. Our rewrites keep the element name verbatim, so we probed phrasing, not the
referring expression that~\cite{groundersunderstand} perturbs; their $84\%$ is the yield of
an adversarial search over Windows desktops rather than a paraphrase failure rate, and the
mobile case they leave open is ours.

\emph{Split composition.} The split is held out by task, not by application. Of the
attributable applications $95.6\%$ also occur in training, and $55\%$ of test steps cannot
be attributed at all, \emph{unknown} and never read as \emph{unseen}. We report no
standalone comparison against an external model, which that overlap would favour. Nor is
task disjointness the same as phrase disjointness, since $17.3\%$ of test references appear
verbatim in a $2.6\%$ slice of training data.

\emph{Scope of the construct.} Every claim is at the level of a system, never of a single
sentence, and the one setting where this family of measures has been validated against
people recommends a reference-based score for ranking systems even while its reference-free
model correlates best per instruction~\cite{zhao2021}. The metric asks whether a grounding
model reaches the right element, not whether a person would, so a system could climb it by
writing for the grounder; we bound that rather than exclude it, a sentence naming a screen
region scoring $89.5\%$ when right and $3.3\%$ when wrong. \emph{Scope.} Every result here is confined to a single
screen, English interfaces, one dataset, one 3B base model and one training configuration.

\section{Conclusion}

We built supervision for GUI instruction generation without human annotation and measured
what it buys on a scale calibrated at both ends. Executability moves from $47.6$ to $59.4$
points at two seeds, against a human-reference level of $75.7$ and a measured floor of
$12.0$, and the sign is preserved under a second grounder trained on different data. Whether naming the
element before writing the sentence adds anything is a question the pre-registration answers
over two seeds per branch, and one run was never executed. At the single seed available the
treatment sits $1.93$ points below its control, inside the band declared inconclusive in
advance, and the registered thresholds put the rest of the outcome space at inconclusive or
negative. The diagnosis is what we carry forward. The prefix is worth $+5.7$ points where
the model identifies the element correctly and $-25$ where it does not, the largest locus of
damage being an action-prior failure fine-tuning introduces before any descriptor exists,
and the preference stage this diagnosis specifies adds $+0.63$ pp over a control matched
update for update, inside that band again. The mechanism is not broken,
since it pays where identification succeeds, but what it is worth is conditional on
identification accuracy, which is where the next experiment must act.

